-- title:
--   At a ramified prime the Hecke set is one coset
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb Q$ and let $q'$ be a prime such that `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q'` holds, i.e. $a<0$, $b<0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra (every nonzero element is a unit) exactly when $q'\in v$. Let $S$ be a $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ which is an Eichler order of level $N$, that is, $S=\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_2$ for maximal orders $\Lambda_1,\Lambda_2$ with relative index $N$ of $S$ in $\Lambda_1$. Then there is a unit $\pi$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ with the following six properties. First, $\pi$ lies in `primeHeckeSet S q'`: $\pi$ lies in the adelic box $\widehat S$ generated by $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in S$ and $\alpha$ an integral finite adele, $q'\pi^{-1}\in\widehat S$, while $\pi^{-1}\notin\widehat S$ and $q'^{-1}\pi\notin\widehat S$. Second, the component of $\pi$ at every height-one prime $w$ not containing $q'$ is $1$. Third, $\pi$ normalises the stabiliser $U=\widehat S^\times$ of the set $\widehat S$ under left multiplication: $u\in U\iff \pi u\pi^{-1}\in U$. Fourth, $\pi^2$ equals the diagonal (central) idele attached to $q'\in\mathbb Q^\times$ times some $u\in U$. Fifth, `primeHeckeSet S q'` is the single left coset $\pi U$. Sixth, the Hecke matrix of this set on the class set $\mathrm{Cl}=\mathbb H^\times\backslash\widehat{\mathbb H}^\times/U$ is the permutation matrix whose $(i,j)$ entry is $1$ if the class of $x_j\pi$ ($x_j$ the chosen representative of $j$) equals $i$, and $0$ otherwise. Finally, for $h\in\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ one has $h\in\pi\widehat S\cap\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ if and only if $h\in S$ and either $h=0$ or $\mathrm{padicValRat}_{q'}(\mathrm{nrd}\,h)\ge 1$.
--
--   This is the local statement at the ramified prime of a definite quaternion algebra: the integral ideals of reduced norm $q'$ form a single class, so the Brandt matrix $B_{q'}$ of an Eichler order is the permutation matrix of the involution $[x]\mapsto[x\pi]$ of the class set, $\pi$ being a uniformiser of the division algebra $\mathbb H\otimes\mathbb Q_{q'}$ and $1$ elsewhere. It is used in the comparison of this Brandt matrix with the Frobenius action on supersingular points in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, and in [`QuaternionAlgebra.IsEichlerOrder.primeHeckeSet_eq_and_heckeKernel_eq_of_ramified`](thm.html#QuaternionAlgebra.IsEichlerOrder.primeHeckeSet_eq_and_heckeKernel_eq_of_ramified).
